<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Essential 1.3 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-linear-inequalities" component="essential" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Linear Inequalities</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Many real situations have limits instead of exact values: a matatu carries at most <m>14</m> passengers, and a pass mark is at least <m>50</m> per cent. Statements like these are inequalities, and you have already used the symbols <m>\lt</m>, <m>\gt</m>, <m>\leq</m> and <m>\geq</m> to compare numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will form inequalities in one unknown from real-life situations and solve simple and compound inequalities. You will then form linear inequalities in two unknowns, represent them as regions on a graph, solve simultaneous linear inequalities graphically, and write down the inequalities that describe a given region.
    </p>
    <p>
      Inequalities let you describe every acceptable choice at once. They are the starting point for planning with limited money, time, land or materials.
    </p>
  </introduction>

